WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.040
YUFENG GUO: Code reuse
is a central tenet

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.510
of software development.

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.400
Machine learning
should be no different.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:08.700
Stay tuned to find out how to
use TensorFlow Hub to easily

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:11.670
load up and customize
state-of-the-art models

00:00:11.670 --> 00:00:13.200
in your TensorFlow code.

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.870
Welcome to "AI
Adventures," where

00:00:23.870 --> 00:00:27.750
we explore the art, science,
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:28.900
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:33.350
And on this episode, we're going
to check out TensorFlow Hub.

00:00:33.350 --> 00:00:36.560
Developing state-of-the-art
machine learning models is no

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.520
easy feat.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:40.100
Large, state-of-the-art models
require lots of carefully

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.400
curated, balanced data to
train, and they take a long time

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:44.920
to train as well.

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:48.650
Now, TensorFlow Hub is a library
for the publication, discovery,

00:00:48.650 --> 00:00:51.680
and consumption of reusable
parts of machine learning

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:52.640
models.

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:55.580
The primary use case
for the models in TF Hub

00:00:55.580 --> 00:00:57.650
is for transfer learning.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.860
Transfer learning is a technique
to base your machine learning

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.930
models on other large models
pre-trained on even larger data

00:01:04.930 --> 00:01:05.970
sets.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:09.950
This enables you to train your
particular customized model

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:14.690
on a smaller data set, improve
generalization, and speed up

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.460
the training process
since you're not

00:01:16.460 --> 00:01:18.740
starting from scratch.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:22.640
Because TF Hub is
integrated with TensorFlow,

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.690
you end up pulling in sections
of a TensorFlow graph.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:29.780
And so the library makes
it super easy to load up

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:32.480
these models to be fine tuned.

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.770
The resources in TF Hub
are known as modules.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:40.370
A module is imported
into a TensorFlow program

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:43.910
by creating a module
object from a string

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:48.614
with its either URL or file
system path that's shown.

00:01:48.614 --> 00:01:50.030
Now, there are
already many models

00:01:50.030 --> 00:01:53.300
available on TF Hub
with more on the way.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:55.880
The two broad categories
of model types

00:01:55.880 --> 00:01:59.470
available today are
images and text.

00:01:59.470 --> 00:02:02.000
The image models are
trained extensively

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.220
to classify objects and images.

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:07.490
And by reusing their feature
detection capabilities,

00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:10.759
you can create models that
recognize your own custom

00:02:10.759 --> 00:02:14.300
classes using much less
training data and time than it

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:17.390
took to make those
more advanced models.

00:02:17.390 --> 00:02:19.610
Some of the image
models available today

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:23.930
include Inception V1,
V2, and V3, Mobile Net

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:27.950
in all sorts of configurations,
NASNet and PNASNet

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.960
as well as Resonant.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.210
Most of these models
were, at one point,

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:34.610
state-of-the-art image
classification models,

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:38.150
and they serve as great starting
points for many image-based

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:40.610
machine learning applications.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:44.110
Now, the text-based models,
they're no slouch either.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:48.860
Currently, there's the universal
sentence encoder, Elmo,

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:51.140
which is a model
train on the 1 billion

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:55.220
word benchmark, NNLM, which
is a neural network language

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:58.400
model trained on the
Google News dataset.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:02.580
So yeah, there's some pretty
cool models on TensorFlow Hub.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:03.190
OK.

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.740
Two final aspects that I want
to cover about TensorFlow Hub

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:09.120
are the guides to using
the modules, which

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.150
are very important,
and how you can publish

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:13.910
your own modules to the hub.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:17.330
First, the TF Hub website
has a number of great guides

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.600
that show you how to load up
and find two models for your use

00:03:20.600 --> 00:03:21.200
case.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:23.360
For example, this
guide here shows

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.390
how to do text classification
of movie reviews

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:29.000
with the NNLM model.

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:31.010
You can take this
and easily swap out

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:35.060
the dataset, the model,
or both to your liking.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:39.830
So TF Hub-- it can't be a hub
without your contribution.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:42.440
As you develop new
and interesting models

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.160
with your own data, consider
creating a TF Hub module

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.020
that others can then reuse.

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.660
You might find collaborators
who can improve upon your models

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.240
even further or discover
use cases that you

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:55.280
hadn't thought of.

00:03:55.280 --> 00:03:58.910
TF Hub is not just the place to
find machine learning models.

00:03:58.910 --> 00:04:02.390
It's an entire framework for
publishing and consuming models

00:04:02.390 --> 00:04:05.870
all with an easy-to-use
and consistent interface.

00:04:05.870 --> 00:04:08.390
This, I believe,
is critical, as I

00:04:08.390 --> 00:04:10.790
think it will enable
us to think in a higher

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:12.620
level of abstraction.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:15.890
This leads to more innovative
ways of remixing and reusing

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:19.760
existing models, expanding
the possibilities of what is

00:04:19.760 --> 00:04:21.800
possible with machine learning.

00:04:21.800 --> 00:04:24.990
Thanks for watching this episode
of "Cloud AI Adventures."

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:26.840
And if you enjoyed
it, please like it

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.110
and subscribe to get all
the latest episodes right

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:31.040
when they come in.

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:33.200
For now, check
out TensorFlow Hub

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:35.840
and see how you can build
on top of some of the most

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:37.906
advanced models around.

